WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.900
So welcome, everyone,
and in my talk,

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.810
I will discuss some aspects
of Shiny open source,

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.300
but before we move
on, let me briefly

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.620
remind you why our Community
praises Shiny so much.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.500
So, first of all, you can
prototype your applications

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.400
very fast.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.640
So you can create
your first POC

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.730
in a matter of hours
rather than days.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.940
That all comes
wrapped in a neat user

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.863
interface powered by bootstrap.

00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:35.780
So you don't really need
to reinvent the wheel

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.320
when it comes to the styling.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.500
Obviously, we can customize
it as it was shown

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.420
in the previous presentations.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.820
Shiny leverages the
power of hardware

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.670
when it comes to
the data analysis.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.610
So in the backend, you can
use those powerful packages

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.750
from the tidyverse family, for
instance, and Shiny also.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.570
And it's nice to integrate
that with a large number

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.320
of visualization packages like
ggplot2, leaflet, Data.Table,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.930
and even more interactivity.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.420
So how different UI
components talk to each other

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.220
is quite easy to understand.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.292
But with all those
advantages in mind,

00:01:10.292 --> 00:01:11.750
sometimes I have
impression that we

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.810
forget that the true power
of Shiny lies in the fact

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.420
that this is fully
open source software.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.880
And I came to
realize that recently

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.270
when I was like reading
again through the blog

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.120
post describing the
winning solutions

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.030
to the second annual
Shiny contest.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.940
And I realized for how
many different applications

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.590
you can actually use Shiny.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:38.180
So here we have an
interactive data storytelling.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.090
We have a machine learning
backed application

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.970
for visualizing
your musical taste.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:48.440
We have data analysis dashboard
that presents the most

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.660
popular GitHub repositories.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.610
And finally, you
can even make games

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.430
as proven by our very own Pedro.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.400
But all of that wouldn't
be really possible

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.040
without all of those,
like Shiny extensions

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.660
that are available now.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.830
And every year
there's even more.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.680
And more of them.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.770
So there is no way that
I could cover all of them

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.530
in detail during
my 10 minute slot.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.870
But I decided to cherry pick
with just a few,

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.680
without which I can't
even start thinking about,

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.550
I starting developing my
own Shiny applications.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:27.680
So let's kick off with
shinyJS, which

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.570
is a package from
Dean Attali that

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:32.240
lets you perform common
useful JavaScript

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.540
operations in Shiny like hiding
elements, disabling inputs,

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.710
resetting values, or alerting
some variables that I often

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:41.690
use for debugging myself.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.910
The cool thing, though,
is that you don't really

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:45.740
need to know any
JavaScript to start

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:49.730
using that because everything
is wrapped in R functions that

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.000
are nicely documented.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.650
So it's quite easy
to get started.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.420
Then we have Shiny
widgets, which is basically

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.320
a collection of extra
components to enhance

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.600
your Shiny applications,
the repository

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.080
contains a big selection of
the components that are usually

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.990
nicely documented with
little code snippets such

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.030
that you can copy
and paste the code

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.490
and adjust it for your own needs.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.240
Some of my favorite
elements are, for example,

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.400
ticker input, those
fancy checkbox button

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.720
or horizontal
multiple-choice selection.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.560
Then we have a small but
rather powerful package,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.530
shinyccsloaders,
that helps you

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.350
with adding a loading
animations to the output

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.210
instead of blanking them out.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:40.350
So I mentioned to you already
that Shiny uses this powerful

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.810
part for data analysis.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:46.020
But but certain actions
can take a while.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.470
So it's better to provide
our user with some feedback

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.150
that there is something
happening in the background.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.030
So you can do it with
simple one function

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:59.460
call withSpinner that
that adds to your Shiny

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.310
those like little animations.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.730
OK, and I wouldn't be myself
if I haven't mentioned

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:09.600
about some packages that help
you style your apps. At Appsilon,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.360
we care a lot about
how the apps look.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.690
So starting from the left,
we have bs4Dash

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:19.560
that provides you with
Bootstrap for admin,

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.140
for your dashboards.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:26.970
Then we have shinyMobile that
helps you create mobile-ready,

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:30.630
Shiny apps that look almost
like native apps for iOS

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:31.740
and Android.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.040
And then on the right, we
have our own shiny.semantic

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.770
that helps you to attach
an alternative look

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.810
to your Shiny applications.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:43.960
OK, and I mentioned that I
just scratched the surface

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:48.820
here, so there is even more
fantastic Shiny extensions

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.000
that you can find on this
Shiny Awesome repository

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.800
that I discovered recently.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:05:00.940
And this little repository
summarizes and categorizes

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.990
various Shiny extensions
such that you can always find

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.370
something that you need there.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:10.230
I encourage you to check it out.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:14.320
OK, but using all those Shiny
extensions is just like one way

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.940
how you can take advantage
of Shiny open source-ness,

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:23.800
but you can help yourself
with building those tools.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.830
So first of all, you
can start from something

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:30.610
as simple as reporting
it back or bringing

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:35.740
some new idea to the table by
creating a new issue on GitHub

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.260
repository or emailing
the author of the code.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.760
But remember to be respectful
with that, as very often

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:47.140
open source code is created by
enthusiasts, or as in our case,

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:49.900
this is something that we
do outside of our daily work

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.550
with clients.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.040
Then if you feel
comfortable enough

00:05:54.040 --> 00:06:00.430
actually to fix some bug or
contribute with a new idea

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:03.550
to a certain package, feel
free to do some coding

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:05.740
and create a new pull request.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:09.670
But remember first about reading
the contributing guidelines

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:13.600
as they usually contain
useful information with what

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.450
to do or not to do on
a given repository.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:19.430
Remember about testing
all of your changes

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.760
so that you see that
you didn't break

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.710
all the previous
functionalities of the code,

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.190
and always ask
politely for a review.

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:29.200
It's good to have
another pair of eyes

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.180
to see to check the
quality of your code.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:39.150
OK, but now if you
still see that there

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:42.150
is no solution to your
problem available,

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:45.330
then you can move on
and think about creating

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:46.480
your own package.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.080
And remember that, for
starters, it doesn't really

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.350
need to be perfect as
some solution is better

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.390
than no solution whatsoever.

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.820
And and always you
may find someone

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.910
in the community who would be
interested in collaborating

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:01.660
and moving the project forward

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:04.890
if you don't really have time to
do all the coding on your own.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.550
And then a couple
of tips from me:

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:11.610
remember, about adding
tests to your package

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:14.130
from the early beginning,
as it will really

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:17.370
spare you so much troubles
at the later stage

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:18.780
of the development.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.910
Remember about documenting
your code. For you

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:24.870
it might be quite obvious how to
use your own functions, but not

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.540
necessarily for the
users of your package.

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:30.330
And recently, it
became a good practice

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:35.280
to add interactive examples
to your repository such

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.390
that users can test

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:40.650
what this package actually
can help them with before

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.870
they can even download it.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:44.240
And then comes the fun part.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:47.640
So remember about publishing
it, sharing with others to get

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.720
credits and acknowledgments.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.480
It's a really satisfying feeling.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:56.350
OK, with that, let me mention
our very own Appsilon open

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:59.080
source family related
to Shiny.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.150
So you have a
variety of packages

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:04.330
here related to
styling packages that

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:08.530
help you with debugging,
translations, and routine.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.800
I will not go into too
much detail here as

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:15.250
we have a separate
prerecorded video available

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:18.910
where I discuss some
of the possibilities

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.120
of those packages, and a
little bit more details.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.740
Just let me mention
here that every year,

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:27.100
we try to extend this least.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.310
So stay tuned as later
on Phillip and Marek

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:35.000
will tell you a little bit more
about our two new projects.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:40.940
OK, so to like prove to you
that our packages can be useful,

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:43.490
a couple of months
ago, we decided

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.600
to challenge ourselves
and try to implement

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.350
a fully functional Shiny
application in less

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:52.850
than 24 hours.

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:55.370
So here you have the
screenshots from two

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:59.060
out of like six entries
to this little competition

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:03.240
for the comprehensive list
of all the amazing solutions

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.890
you can check our blog post.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:09.050
And if you can't
wait and you still

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:12.590
want to see all of our
packages in action,

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:16.040
I encourage you to visit
the shiny.tools website

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.930
that we set up recently.

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:21.680
And it contains
references to the GitHub,

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:27.000
to the documentation, and live
examples of all R packages.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.720
So we can test
them if they can be

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:33.100
useful for your
Shiny development.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:35.240
OK, so that's all I get.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:36.890
Thank you very much
for your attention.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:39.910
Feel free to bring me if
you have any questions,

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:42.350
and I hope you will enjoy
the rest of the session.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:43.600
Cheers

